<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap ab-why">
    <div>
      <h2>Why the forum exists</h2>
      <p>Nearly everyone who fishes, processes or sells fish in Sri Lanka works in the small-scale sector. Yet the sector is threatened by overfishing, unregulated technology, the collapse of fisheries co-operatives and, increasingly, climate change.</p>
      <p>Research in Sri Lanka has focused on fish biology and oceanography, not on the people who fish: their rights, their livelihoods and their exclusion from decisions. The forum was created to close that gap.</p>
    </div>
    <blockquote class="ab-quote">
      <p>Small-scale fisheries make an important contribution to nutrition, food security, sustainable livelihoods and poverty alleviation. Despite this, the issues constraining their sustainable development remain poorly understood.</p>
      <footer>Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations</footer>
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tide">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What we set out to do</h2>
      <p>The aims in the forum’s founding blueprint fall into three areas.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ab-aims">
      <article>
        <h3>Share knowledge</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Run platforms where everyone involved can share information on small-scale fisheries</li>
          <li>Promote joint research</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Shape policy</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Voice the needs of fishing communities</li>
          <li>Advise government and provide policy inputs</li>
          <li>Help develop and implement a national fisheries policy</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Connect globally</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Bring the FAO SSF Guidelines into national law, policy and civil society work</li>
          <li>Link with regional and international bodies such as Too Big To Ignore and ICSF</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--navy">
  <div class="wrap ab-how">
    <figure class="ab-how__img">
      <img src="/assets/img/fgd-negombo.webp" alt="Members of the Negombo fishing community seated in a hall during a consultation" width="1000" height="664" loading="lazy">
    </figure>
    <div>
      <h2>How we work</h2>
      <div class="ab-how__item">
        <h3>A rights-based approach</h3>
        <p>Fishing communities, women and men alike, should take part in the decisions that affect them and share responsibility for the resource. We give particular weight to vulnerable and marginalised groups.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ab-how__item">
        <h3>Interactive platforms</h3>
        <p>Fishers, officials, scientists, industry and civil society meet on neutral ground, usually a university, to work through a single issue and agree recommendations for policy makers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ab-how__item">
        <h3>Research and outreach</h3>
        <p>Members publish research, write for the national press and run projects directly with fishing communities.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
  <div class="ab-ssf">
    <div>
      <h2>The SSF Guidelines</h2>
      <p>The FAO Voluntary Guidelines for Securing Sustainable Small-Scale Fisheries recognise fishers’ rights and call for management that is participatory, inclusive and holistic. Guidelines only work when governments adopt them, so since 2018 the forum has briefed state officers and consulted fishing communities in the North, East, West and South.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="btn btn--line" href="https://www.fao.org/voluntary-guidelines-small-scale-fisheries/en/" rel="noopener">Read the guidelines on fao.org</a>
  </div>
  </div>
</section>

